{"title":"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Part_A:_Each_correct_answer_is_worth_5_points\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Part A: Each correct answer is worth 5 points</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Problem_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problem_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Problem_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Problem_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Problem_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Problem_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Problem_7\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 7</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Problem_8\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 8</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Problem_9\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 9</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Problem_10\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 10</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Part_B:_Each_correct_answer_is_worth_6_points\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Part B: Each correct answer is worth 6 points</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Problem_11\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 11</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Problem_12\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 12</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Problem_13\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 13</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Problem_14\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 14</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Problem_15\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 15</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Problem_16\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 16</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#Problem_17\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 17</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-20\"><a href=\"#Problem_18\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 18</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-21\"><a href=\"#Problem_19\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 19</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-22\"><a href=\"#Problem_20\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 20</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-23\"><a href=\"#Part_C:_Each_correct_answer_is_worth_8_points\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Part C: Each correct answer is worth 8 points</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-24\"><a href=\"#Problem_21\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 21</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-25\"><a href=\"#Problem_22\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 22</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-26\"><a href=\"#Problem_23\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 23</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-27\"><a href=\"#Problem_24\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 24</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-28\"><a href=\"#Problem_25\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 25</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-29\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h1><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Part_A:_Each_correct_answer_is_worth_5_points\">Part A: Each correct answer is worth 5 points</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Part A: Each correct answer is worth 5 points\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h1>\n<ul><li>If any diagrams are missing, consult the pdf: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.cemc.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests/1998/1998Gauss7Contest.pdf\">https://www.cemc.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests/1998/1998Gauss7Contest.pdf</a></li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_1\">Problem 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34e10d57253570acd84d5370c102c64ae1e3aedd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1998 - 998}{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"88\" height=\"38\" /> is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/3/7e3c743a90527f9caeb5753dba607ad86f714170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 1 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 1000 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 0.1 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 10 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 0.001$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"412\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 1\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_2\">Problem 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/9/129aa112f3e1d4eb1f1ee2b65225adf5d64173dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4567$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> is tripled. The ones digit (units digit) in the resulting number is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/3/4e35ce0f62c2eb14473a59945f0dafbe23d62998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 5 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 6 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 7 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 3 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"329\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_2\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 2\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\">Problem 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/e/0ae4a806ec3e6a748f7eee9bf4045ea3ed3faae2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S = 6\\times 10,000 + 5\\times 1,000 + 4\\times 10 + 3\\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"352\" height=\"16\" />, what is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/7/a57905c0bb6bb4fdbb72e84b0a201b24f7e16399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 6,543 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 65,043 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 65,431 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 65,403 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 60,541$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"546\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 3\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_4\">Problem 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Jean writes five tests and achieves the marks shown on the graph. What is her average mark on these five tests?\n</p><p>[insert bar graph with 5 bars: 80, 70, 60, 90, 80]\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/c/8dcc97a5e85356be551da2af876d6a19a640ca3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 74 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 76 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 70 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 64 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 79$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"375\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 4\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_5\">Problem 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If a machine produces 150 items in one minute, how many would it produce in 10 seconds?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/b/a9b212e7d658c0bda2a1690b954c3e508d989e0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 10 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 15 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 20 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 25 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 30$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"378\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 5\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_6\">Problem 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the multiplication question, the sum of the digits in the four boxes is:\n</p><p>[Multiply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/b/9cbee9e868bbc193f2a17c214f04050022d39953.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$879 \\times 492$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"76\" height=\"13\" /> using long multiplication. Find the sum of the four numbers in the thousands place column.]\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/4/994990824d34f76aeabb4edc58ce61dab34fe895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 13 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 12 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 27 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 9 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 22$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"366\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 6\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_7\">Problem 7</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 7\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A rectangular field is 80 m long and 60 m wide. If fence posts are placed at the corners and are 10 m apart along the 4 sides of the field, how many posts are needed to completely fence the field?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/9/639afd5b1362fafa0ebc7993bf065b6cf242817e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 24 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 26 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 28 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 30 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"375\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 7\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_8\">Problem 8</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 8\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Tuesday’s high temperature was 4 C warmer than that of Monday’s. Wednesday’s high temperature\nwas 6 C cooler than that of Monday’s. If Tuesday’s high temperature was 22 C, what was\nWednesday’s high temperature?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/3/d135616355bb771dce1b4fc690526c8b0c4edee1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)} 20 \\quad\\text{(B)} 24\\quad \\text{(C)} 12\\quad \\text{(D)} 32 \\quad \\text{(E)} 16 \\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"281\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><br />\n(all in Celsius)\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 8\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_9\">Problem 9</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 9\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two numbers have a sum of 32. If one of the numbers is – 36, what is the other number?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1dd5a9fcbf006501f451b00a11ce8c65c34097.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 68 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ -4 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 4 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 72 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ -68$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"402\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 9\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_10\">Problem 10</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 10\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>At the waterpark, Bonnie and Wendy decided to race each other down a waterslide. Wendy won by 0.25 seconds. If Bonnie’s time was exactly 7.80 seconds, how long did it take for Wendy to go down the slide?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/6/ed6e21ab46cd256952ec22a43b0bb4c02fc59a8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 7.8 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 8.05 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 7.55 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 7.15 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 7.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"428\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 10\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h1><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Part_B:_Each_correct_answer_is_worth_6_points\">Part B: Each correct answer is worth 6 points</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Part B: Each correct answer is worth 6 points\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h1>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_11\">Problem 11</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 11\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Kalyn cut rectangle R from a sheet of paper. A smaller rectangle is then cut from the large rectangle R to produce figure S. In comparing R to S,\n</p><p>[R is a rectangle with sides 8 and 6 cm. S is the same as R with a 4x1 rectangle cut from one of its corners.]\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/b/03b69432d42563d57639078567d60e8663f838cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A) the area and perimeter both decrease}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"302\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2553e581f41871cdba10e7981447d7b172188bba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(B) the area decreases and the perimeter increases}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"366\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/d/03d3657f4aebe13bd760a839b1c8424117745faa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(C) the area and perimeter both increase}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"299\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/7/ea79b1ee18228035064cab3775ea740bcac77ea3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(D) the area increases and the perimeter decreases}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"368\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/9/ec9ee81c22fe6f10a76a556592fbdb43afa2dd8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(E) the area decreases and the perimeter stays the same}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"409\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 11\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_12\">Problem 12</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 12\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Steve plants ten trees every three minutes. If he continues planting at the same rate, how long will it take him to plant 2500 trees?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/c/e5c95c530b8764df269749b57c25f74661701a41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 1 \\dfrac{1}{4} \\text{h} \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 3 \\text{h} \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 5 \\text{h} \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 10 \\text{h} \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 12 \\dfrac{1}{2} \\text{h}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"421\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 12\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_13\">Problem 13</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 13\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The pattern of figures (triangle, dark circle, square, dark triangle, circle) is repeated over and over again. The 214th figure in the sequence is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/7/557c259314512c120a30d2f14d30b5261fce30ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A) triangle}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/4/834b0cda9c74da4888588a398d9becee64a29f6d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(B) dark circle}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/d/15db520232b165272ced979b271cbbc159366805.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(C) square}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/4/ee43f331b49ceff8a8ff79acfa691770bb1b77c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(D) dark triangle}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"124\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/f/0bfbbb7934d96150a37259a07b53865646744386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(E) circle}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 13\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_14\">Problem 14</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=16\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 14\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A cube has a volume of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/3/523c7d52aacfb9bfea1996485cb75e33e307aee3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125 \\text{cm}^3.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" /> What is the area of one face of the cube?\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/8/2e813434524a17ff7bdc8b52d1511ca474fc8f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 20 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 25 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 41 \\dfrac{2}{3} \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 5 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 75 \\text{cm}^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"522\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 14\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_15\">Problem 15</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=17\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 15\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The diagram shows a magic square in which the sums of the numbers in any row, column or diagonal are equal. What is the value of n?\n</p><p>[A 3x3 magic square grid is shown. 8 is in the 1st row 1st column. 9 is in the 2nd row 1st column. 4 is in the 2nd row 3rd column. 4 is in the 3rd row 1st column. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is in the 3rd row 2nd column.]\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/f/e8fade1995950cd3108604cf44cfb6e999140851.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 3 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 6 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 7 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 10 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"347\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 15\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_16\">Problem 16</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=18\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 16\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each of the digits 3, 5, 6, 7, and 8 is placed one to a box in the diagram. If the two digit number is subtracted from the three digit number, what is the smallest difference?\n</p><p>[Align three boxes to the right and two boxes below so it looks like a three digit number subtracting a two digit number.]\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/a/dba22254d16a5cfdfab34d5ea4dd4b01f6f203f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 269 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 278 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 484 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 271 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 261$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"420\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 16\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_17\">Problem 17</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=19\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 17\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Claire takes a square piece of paper and folds it in half four times without unfolding, making an\nisosceles right triangle each time. After unfolding the paper to form a square again, the creases on the\npaper would look like:\n</p><p>(see attached pdf for diagrams)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/2/0e248fa9bd73d8eff20e541a1b615da527055391.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\  \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\  \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\  \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\  \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"288\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 17\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_18\">Problem 18</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=20\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 18\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The letters of the word ‘GAUSS’ and the digits in the number ‘1998’ are each cycled separately and then numbered as shown:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/f/a6f58edb19c374417ed58691a39899c773cc2337.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1. \\quad AUSSG \\qquad 9981\\]\" width=\"164\" height=\"13\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/3/173c9dc7fc07c87198127879628ed6e063393c17.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2. \\quad USSGA \\qquad 9819\\]\" width=\"165\" height=\"13\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/7/4b78a41bb799de990438a05236364b2a0a8c8a1a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3. \\quad SSGAU \\qquad 8199\\]\" width=\"165\" height=\"13\" />\nIf the pattern continues in this way, what number will appear in front of GAUSS 1998?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8137bb596c75af52966df5fa1644bd631979eda1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 4 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 5 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 9 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 16 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"348\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 18\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_19\">Problem 19</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=21\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 19\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Juan and Mary play a two-person game in which the winner gains 2 points and the loser loses 1 point. If Juan won exactly 3 games and Mary had a final score of 5 points, how many games did they play?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/c/57cfc5b81b87ea5e8af046215b65c3ca04c33f73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 7 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 8 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 4 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 5 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"338\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 19\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_20\">Problem 20</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=22\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 20\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each of the 12 edges of a cube is coloured either red or green. Every face of the cube has at least one\nred edge. What is the smallest number of red edges?\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/9/c0904a1807339bc66742fe6716ebc810957bf718.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 2 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 3 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 4 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 5 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"330\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 20\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h1><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Part_C:_Each_correct_answer_is_worth_8_points\">Part C: Each correct answer is worth 8 points</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=23\" title=\"Edit section: Part C: Each correct answer is worth 8 points\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h1>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_21\">Problem 21</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=24\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 21\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Ten points are spaced equally around a circle. How many different chords can be formed by joining\nany 2 of these points? (A chord is a straight line joining two points on the circumference of a circle.)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/f/fef70b4a18cc6ef62feaae2c8de18156351027e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 9 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 45 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 17 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 66 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 55$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"366\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 21\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_22\">Problem 22</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=25\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 22\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each time a bar of soap is used, its volume decreases by 10%. What is the minimum number of times\na new bar would have to be used so that less than one-half its volume remains?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c546ed5f60b14548fdd6b75885ffb093a0a17af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 5 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 6 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 7 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 8 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"330\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 22\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_23\">Problem 23</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=26\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 23\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A cube measures 10 by 10 by 10 cm. Three cuts are made parallel to the faces of the cube as shown (in three perpendicular directions) creating eight separate solids which are then separated. What is the increase in the total surface area?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/b/59bcfe6832a1a3a31014c9f687577f8e248901af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 300 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 800 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 1200 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 600 \\text{cm}^2 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 0 \\text{cm}^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"554\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 23\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_24\">Problem 24</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=27\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 24\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>On a large piece of paper, Dana creates a “rectangular spiral” by drawing line segments of lengths, in cm, of 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, ... as shown. Dana’s pen runs out of ink after the total of all the lengths he has drawn is 3000 cm. What is the length of the longest line segment that Dana draws?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/1/031312eb2a8666378710aacc1b2d30bf00c06988.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 38 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 39 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 54 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 55 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\  50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"375\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 24\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_25\">Problem 25</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=28\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 25\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two natural numbers, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/b/34b2864f06b4e50859a0bea1a812d50a2154547b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" /> do not end in zero. The product of any pair, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/b/34b2864f06b4e50859a0bea1a812d50a2154547b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" /> is a power of 10 (that is, 10, 100, 1000, 10 000 , ...). If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b6051ac0a556f98f7481e62093ba35754d8805.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p &gt; q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> , the last digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/f/a1f8131b9cef2c23369011c1cbc425f2861c026e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p-q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> cannot be\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/3/0b324531c4e9aa97effe0399a23e0369c7a04803.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 1 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 3 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 5 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 7 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"330\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 25\">Solution</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;section=29\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First Competition</b>\n